Let $PD=a$ $PE=b$ $PF=c$ and the center of the sphere be $O$ From using power of a point on the three pairwise cross sections we obtain $$\sqrt{6}a=\sqrt{10}b=\sqrt{15}c=k$$ where $k$ is some constant By Stewart's Theorem on $\triangle{OBE}$ \begin{align*} \sqrt{10}b(b+\sqrt{10})+OP^2(b+\sqrt{10}) &= 10b+10\sqrt{10} \\ \sqrt{10}b+OP^2 &= 10 \\ OP^2 &= 10 k \end{align*} 
 Lemma In a sphere of radius $r$ with center $O$ there are three pairwise perpendicular chords that are concurrent at a point $P$ Given that $P$ divides the chords into six segments of lengths $a$ $b$ $c$ $d$ $e$ $f$ the following holds $$a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2+e^2+f^2=6r^2 2OP^2$$ 
 To prove this consider the cross section of two chords Drop the perpendiculars from the center of the circle (the cross section) to each chord and use Pythagorean Theorem From our lemma we obtain \begin{align*} 6+10+15+a^2+b^2+c^2 &= 60 2OP^2 \\ 31 + \frac{k^2}{6} + \frac{k^2}{10}+\frac{k^2}{15} &= 60 2(10 k) \\ k &= 9 \end{align*} Therefore the volume of $DEFP$ is $$\frac{1}{6}abc = \frac{1}{6} \cdot \frac{9}{\sqrt{6}} \cdot \frac{9}{\sqrt{10}} \cdot \frac{9}{\sqrt{15}} = \boxed{\frac{81}{20}}$$

\begin{align*}
a^2+b^2=c^2 &\implies (a+b)^2 2ab=c^2 \\
&\implies (a+b)^2 2\cdot8=12^2\\
&\implies a+b=4\sqrt{10}\\
&\implies a+b+c=\boxed{12+4\sqrt{10}} \end{align*}

