If we let the side lengths of the original rectangle be $x$ and $y$ and the angle of the direction that the rectangle is translated in be $\theta $ then it is not difficult to find that the area of the hexagon will be 
 \begin{align*}
xy+2x\cos(\theta)+2y\sin(\theta) \end{align*} We know that $xy=20 $ and by the Cauchy Schwarz Inequality we know that 
 \begin{align*}
(x\cos(\theta)+y\sin(\theta))^2 &\le (x^2+y^2)(\cos^2(\theta)+\sin^2(\theta)) \\
&= 7^2 \cdot 1 \\
&= 49 \end{align*} from which it follows that $$x\cos(\theta)+y\sin(\theta) \le 7 $$ Hence the answer is 
 \begin{align*}
20 + 2 \cdot 7 &= 20 + 14 \\
&= \boxed{34} \end{align*} $\square$

Note that the problem can be rephrased as finding the expected number of minutes until all $5$ people have a single cookie left If there are $1 2 3 $ or $4$ people that have a single cookie the probability of the next minute increasing the number of people with a single cookie is $\tfrac{2}{5} \tfrac{3}{5} \tfrac{3}{5} $ and $\tfrac{2}{5} $ respectively Thus the answer is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{5}{2} + \frac{5}{3} + \frac{5}{3} + \frac{5}{2} &= \boxed{\frac{25}{3}} \end{align*} $\square$

First of all the height of the trapezoid is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{880 \cdot 2}{20+24} &= \frac{44 \cdot 40}{44} \\
&= 40 \end{align*} By similar triangles we find that the base of the desired triangle has a length of $$\frac{1}{12} \cdot 24 = 2 $$ and the height of this triangle is obviously just $$\frac{1}{2} \cdot 40 = 20 $$ Hence by the base height triangle area formula the answer is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{2 \cdot 20}{2} &= \boxed{20} \end{align*} $\square$

Let the temperature in Boston be $x^\circ$ Celcius Then the answer is 
 \begin{align*}
1 8 \cdot (x+10) + 32 (1 8 \cdot x + 32)
&= 1 8x + 18 + 32 1 8x 32 \\
&= \boxed{18}
\end{align*} degrees Fahrenheit $\square$

By Similar Triangles we have $$\frac{AE}{QF} = \frac{PE}{PF} \rightarrow \frac{AE}{12} = \frac{5}{3} \rightarrow AE=20 $$ Since $E$ is the midpoint of $\overline{AB} $ we have $$CF=EB=AE=12 $$ so 
 \begin{align*}
CQ &= CF QF\\
&=20 12\\
&=\boxed{8} \end{align*}

We wish to find ten times the difference between the distances between the top of the Green Building and the intersection of the ziplines and between the top of the Strata Center and the intersection of the ziplines First note that the distance from the ground to the intersection of the ziplines is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\tfrac{1}{160}+\tfrac{1}{90}} &= \frac{1}{\tfrac{5}{288}} \\
&= \frac{288}{5} \end{align*} Therefore by the Pythagorean Theorem and similar triangles the desired quantity is 
 \begin{align*}
10 \cdot \left(200\left(1 \frac{\tfrac{288}{5}}{160}\right) 150\left(1 \frac{\tfrac{288}{5}}{90}\right)\right) &= \boxed{740} \end{align*} $\square$

First note that if $N \equiv 0 \pmod{10} $ then $M$ can be equal $\tfrac{1}{10}N $ giving $$\frac{M}{N} = \frac{1}{10} $$ Otherwise we can let $N=\overline{A}0\overline{B} $ where $A$ and $B$ are the positive integers to the left and right of the $0$ that is removed and $B$ has $k$ digits Then it follows that $$\frac{M}{N} = \frac{10^kA+B}{10^{k+1}A+B} $$ We have 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{10^kA+B}{10^{k+1}A+B} &> \frac{10^kA+\tfrac{B}{10}}{10^{k+1}A+B} \\
&= \frac{1}{10} \end{align*} so $\tfrac{1}{10}$ is indeed the minimum Then for the maximum note that 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{10^kA+B}{10^{k+1}A+B} &= \frac{1}{10} + \frac{\tfrac{9B}{10}}{10^{k+1}A+B} \\
&= \frac{1}{10} + \frac{9}{10^{k+2}\tfrac{A}{B}+10} \end{align*} In order to maximize this we wish to minimize $\tfrac{A}{B} $ which clearly occurs at $A=1 B=10^k 1 $ In this case we have 
 \begin{align*}
10^{k+2}\frac{A}{B} &= 10^{k+2} \cdot \frac{1}{10^k 1} \\
&= \frac{10^{k+2}}{10^k 1} \end{align*} which approaches $100$ as $k$ approaches infinity but it is always less than $100 $ Hence the (unattainable) upper bound for $\tfrac{M}{N}$ is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{10} + \frac{9}{100+10} &= \frac{1}{10} + \frac{9}{110} \\
&= \frac{2}{11} \end{align*} Therefore the smallest interval containing all possible values of $\tfrac{M}{N}$ is $$\left[\frac{1}{10} \frac{2}{11}\right) $$ implying that the answer is $$1000+1000+20+11=\boxed{2031} $$ $\square$

Solution If we make the substitution $x \to \frac{1}{x}$ then we get $f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) + \frac{1}{x}f(x) = \frac{1}{x}$ 

 So then we have the system 
 \begin{align*}
f(x)+xf\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)&=x\\
 \frac{1}{x}f(x) + f\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) &=\frac{1}{x}
\end{align*} 


 Multiplying the second relation by $x$ yields $f(x) + xf\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = 1$ But from the first relation we know that this is $x$ so we must have that $\boxed{x=1}$ 

