 If $a<b$ then $a\diamond b =(b a)[(a 1)\diamond (b 1)] $ 

 Find a general formula for $x\diamond y$ assuming that $y\ge x>0$

Removing from my feed if you need me to edit something please PM me To justify the existence of this post here's the solution to 7 the most immature problem I've seen in a while * an SMT organizer who said "oops we're short on problems" and decided to wrap $\zeta(2)=\tfrac{\pi^2}{6}$ into a convoluted intimidating problem—equivalently an elementary schooler who just learned about the aforementioned equation and overexcitedly turned it into an "original" "thought provoking" problem 
 \begin{align*}
\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\left(\sum_{j=1}^\infty(1+k)^{ j}\right)^2\right)^{ t}}&=\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{k^2}\right)^{ t}}\\
&=\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\frac{\pi^2}{6}\right)^{ t}}\\
&=\sqrt{6\cdot\frac{1}{\frac{\pi^2}{6}}}=\sqrt{\frac{36}{\pi^2}}=\boxed{\frac{6}{\pi}} \end{align*}

To maximize laziness the trick for these kinds of problems is to check the extremes as in a symmetric inequality Here those extremes are adding all the rows first (cp $x=k$ $y=z=\dots=0$ ) and adding the rows and columns in alternating fashion (cp $x=y=\dots$ ) The former costs $19\cdot1^2+19\cdot20^2=7619$ the latter costs 
 \begin{align*}
1^2+2^2+2^2+3^2+\dots+19^2+20^2&=2(1^2+\dots+20^2) (1^2+20^2)\\
&=\tfrac{20\cdot21\cdot41}{3} (1+400)\\
&=5740 401=\boxed{5339} \end{align*}

The condition implies $\angle CAB = \angle ABE$ However notice that $\angle EBA = \angle ABD$ due the definition of reflections Therefore $\angle BAC = \angle ABC$ so $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles with $BC = 6$ Let $T = \angle BAC$ then $2T = \angle EBC$ By Law of Cosines we have that 
 \begin{align*}
6^2 = 3^2 + 6^2 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 6 \cdot \cos \left(T\right) \quad \longrightarrow \quad \cos \left(T\right) = \tfrac{1}{4} \end{align*} By Angle Bisector Theorem \begin{align*}
\dfrac{AB}{BD} = \dfrac{AC}{CD} \quad \longrightarrow \quad \dfrac{3}{BD} = \dfrac{6}{CD} \quad \longrightarrow \quad \dfrac{3}{BD} = \dfrac{6}{6 BD} \quad \longrightarrow \quad BD = 2 \text{ }CD = 4 \end{align*} Thus $BE = BD = 2$ Notice $\sin\left(T\right) = \tfrac{\sqrt{15}}{4}$ so $\cos\left(2T\right) = \cos^2\left(T\right) \sin^2\left(T\right) = \tfrac{7}{8}$ By Law of Cosines we have \begin{align*}
CE = \sqrt{BC^2 + BE^2 2 \cdot BC \cdot BE \cdot \cos \left(2T\right)} = \sqrt{6^2 + 2^2 2 \cdot 6 \cdot 2 \cdot \tfrac{7}{8}} = \boxed{\sqrt{61}} \end{align*}

The probability that $n$ balls are in the box given that the first ball chosen was black is $\frac{1}{101} \cdot \frac{n}{100} \div \frac{1}{2} = \frac{n}{50 \cdot 101}$ Hence the probability that there are $n$ balls and the second ball that rolls out is black given that the first ball that rolls out is black is $\frac{n}{50 \cdot 101} \cdot \frac{n 1}{99} = \frac{1}{50 \cdot 99 \cdot 101} \cdot n(n 1)$ Therefore the desired probability is 
 \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{50 \cdot 99 \cdot 101} \cdot \sum_{n=0}^{100} (n^2 n) &= \frac{1}{50\cdot99\cdot101}\cdot\left(\frac{100\cdot101\cdot201}{6} \frac{100\cdot101}{2}\right) \\ &= \frac{2}{3}
\end{align*} Hence $p+q = \boxed{5}$

Notice that the probability that Jackson is at position $k+1$ after $n$ steps is $\binom{n}{k} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^k$ Hence \begin{align*}
E_n &= \sum_{k=0}^n \frac{1}{k+1} \cdot \binom{n}{k} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^k \\ &= \sum_{k=0}^n \frac{n!}{(k+1)! \cdot (n k)!} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^k \\ &= \frac{1}{n+1} \cdot \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n+1}{k+1} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^k \\ &= \frac{1}{0 15 \cdot (n+1)} \cdot \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n+1}{k+1} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^{k+1} \\ &= \frac{1}{0 15 \cdot (n+1)} \cdot \left( 0 85^{n+1} + \sum_{k= 1}^n \binom{n+1}{k+1} \cdot 0 85^{n k} \cdot 0 15^{k+1} \right) \\ &= \frac{1}{0 15 \cdot (n+1)} \cdot \left( 0 85^{n+1} + 1 \right) \\ &= \frac{1 0 85^{n+1}}{0 15 \cdot (n+1)} \\ &= \frac{20^{n+1} 17^{n+1}}{3 \cdot 20^n \cdot (n+1)}
\end{align*} Notice that $n$ is going to be really large so we can get rid of the $17^{n+1}$ term $\frac{20^{n+1} 17^{n+1}}{3 \cdot 20^n \cdot (n+1)} \approx \frac{20}{3 \cdot (n+1)}$ Then we have $\frac{3n+3}{20} > 2017 \implies n >13445 \overline{6}$ Hence $n=\boxed{13446}$

Repeatedly applying long division we see that 
 \begin{align*}
\left\lfloor \frac{5^{2017015}}{5^{2015}+7} \right\rfloor &= 5^{2017015 2015} \frac{7 \cdot 5^{2017015 2015}}{5^{2015}+7} \\ &= 5^{2017015 2015} 7 \cdot 5^{2017015 2 \cdot 2015} + \frac{7^2 \cdot 5^{2017015 2 \cdot 2015}}{5^{2015}+7} \\ &\vdots \\ &= 5^{2017015 2015} 7 \cdot 5^{2017015 2\cdot 2015} + \cdots + 7^{1000} \frac{7^{1001}}{5^{2015} + 7} 
\end{align*} Therefore $\left\lfloor \frac{5^{2017015}}{5^{2015}+7} \right\rfloor \equiv 1 + \sum_{k=0}^{1000} ( 7)^{1000 k} \cdot 5^{2015k} \pmod{100}$ Clearly this is equivalent to $7^{1000} 1 \equiv 1 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{125}$ and $ 1+ \sum_{k=0}^{1000} 5^{2015k} \equiv 1 +\sum_{k=0}^{1000} 5^{6k} \equiv 1 + 1001 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}$ Hence the desired answer is $\boxed{0}$

It is well known that for any polynomial $P(x)$ with integer coefficients we have $a b \mid P(a) P(b)$ for all integers $a b$ If $P(2016) = x$ we have $2016 1 \mid x 2$ $2016 2 \mid x 3$ and $2016 3 \mid x 2016$ Hence we have 
 \begin{align*}
x &\equiv 2 \pmod{2015} \\ x &\equiv 3 \pmod{2014} \\ x &\equiv 3 \pmod{2013}
\end{align*} The last two equivalences imply that $x \equiv 3 \pmod{2014 \cdot 2013}$ so let $x = 3 + 2014 \cdot 2013y$ Taking 
 $\pmod{2015}$ we have $x \equiv 3+2y \equiv 2 \pmod{2015}$ so the minimum value of $y$ is $1007$ Hence $x \equiv 3+ 2014 \cdot 2013 \cdot 1007 \equiv 3+6 \cdot 1007 \equiv \boxed{2013} \pmod{2016}$ 

 Note that $P(x)=2+(x 1) \cdot (1006 \cdot (x 2)+1)$ satisfies these conditions

It's easy to see that Shaq obtains the largest number when he repeatedly preforms the operation on the two largest numbers currently on the board Note that $[a b] = ab a b = (a 1)(b 1) 1$ so 
 \begin{align*}
V &= [ \cdots [[[100 99] 98] 97] \cdots ] 2] \\ &= \left( \cdots \left(\left(\left(\left(\left(\left(\left(101 1\right)\left(100 1\right) 1\right) 1\right)\left(99 1\right) 1\right) 1\right)\left(98 1\right) 1\right) 1\right) \cdots \right) 1\\ &= \left( \cdots \left(\left(\left(\left(\left(\left(100\cdot99 1\right) 1\right)\cdot98 1\right) 1\right)\cdot97 1\right) 1\right) \cdots \right) 1 \\ &= \left( \cdots \left(\left(\left(100\cdot99 2\right)\cdot98 2\right)\cdot97 2\right) \cdots \right) 1 \\ &= 100! 2\left(98!+97!+\cdots+1!\right) 1
\end{align*} Clearly $N=100$ so we wish to find the integer closes to 
 \begin{align*}
10^6\cdot2\cdot\left(\frac{1}{100\cdot99}+\frac{1}{100\cdot99\cdot98}+\frac{1}{100\cdot99\cdot97\cdot96}+\cdots+\frac{1}{100!}\right) \frac{1}{100!}
\end{align*} which we can approximate as $10^6\cdot2\cdot\left(\frac{1}{100\cdot99}+\frac{1}{100\cdot99\cdot98}\right) \approx 202+2 = \boxed{204}$

Notice that 
 \begin{align*}
f\left(x\right) f\left(\tfrac{1}{x}\right) &= \sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{b \ge 0} \frac{x^{2^a3^b}}{1 x^{6\cdot2^a3^b}} \frac{x^{ 2^a3^b}}{1 x^{ 6\cdot2^a3^b}} \\ &=\sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{b \ge 0}\frac{\left(x^{2^a3^b}+x^{5\cdot2^a3^b}\right)\left(1 x^{ 6\cdot2^a3^b}\right)}{\left(1 x^{6\cdot2^a3^b}\right)\left(1 x^{ 6\cdot2^a3^b}\right)} \\ &= \sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{b \ge 0} \frac{x^{2^a3^b}\cdot\left(1+x^{4\cdot2^a3^b}\right)}{1 x^{6\cdot2^a3^b}} \\&=\sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{b \ge 0} \left(1+x^{4\cdot2^a3^b}\right)\sum_{c\ge0}x^{\left(6c+1\right)\cdot2^a3^b} \\ &= \sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{b \ge 0} \left(\sum_{c\ge0}^{ }x^{\left(6c+1\right)\cdot2^a3^b}+\sum_{c\ge0}^{ }x^{\left(6c+5\right)\cdot2^a3^b}\right) \\ &= \sum_{a\ge 0} \sum_{m \ge 0} x^{2^a \cdot (2m+1)} \\ &= \sum_{n > 0} x^n \\ &= \frac{x}{x 1}
\end{align*} Hence $\tfrac{y}{y 1} = 2016$ so $y = \tfrac{2016}{2017} \implies p+q = \boxed{4033}$

